name=bitewrite
version=4.0
desc=A page where every letter lands like the launcher's — write, notes, code that runs, lyrics that play, typing speed
category=visual
author=GLITCH-BITE-404
exec=run
deps=qml6:qt6-declarative python3:python py:numpy:python-numpy
usage=bitewrite   ·   opens a window · F1 write · F2 notes · F3 code · F4 lyrics · F5 speed · F6 settings
nopreview=bitewrite opens a window — there is nothing to sample into a text pane. Press Enter on it in INSTALLED to open the real thing.
keys=F1:WRITE — free writing, kept for next time|F2:NOTES — as many notes as you like, saved as you type|F3:CODE — JavaScript, Python or Bash, run with Ctrl+Enter|F4:LYRICS — type a song and it plays with you|F5:SPEED — a typing test, wpm live at the bottom|F6:settings — animations, sounds, direction, everything|Ctrl+Enter:run what you wrote / play-pause the song|Esc or ■ stop:CODE — stop a running program (keys and mouse go to it)|Ctrl+F:CODE — finish the program and edit it|Ctrl+N:new note / another program, song or text|Ctrl+K:NOTES — find a note|Alt+↑↓:NOTES — switch notes|Ctrl+Shift+Del:NOTES — delete this note (press twice)|Tab / Shift+Tab:SPEED — change the time / the text|Ctrl+R:start this one over|Ctrl+H:hide the ghost|Ctrl+S:save a copy to Documents|Ctrl+L:clear|Ctrl+= Ctrl+-:bigger / smaller|Ctrl+M:mute|Esc:back to WRITE, or close
about=The launcher's search box is the most satisfying thing in the shell to type into, and it only ever holds one short line. This is that box turned into a whole page, and then into five things to do with it. It writes Hebrew and Arabic right to left — an English word inside a Hebrew line still reads correctly, brackets face the right way — and Chinese, Japanese and emoji take two cells. Every letter is its own animated object. By default it uses the launcher's exact pop (grows from a third of its size with the same OutBack overshoot, rises ten pixels, fades in over 140ms), and the settings swap that for drop, glitch, stamp, spin, float or random. Backspace can shrink letters away, drop them off the page, burst them into dust, flick them sideways or tear them apart. There are nine sound packs. launcher is the shell's own click at your sfx volume. The other eight are synthesised on first run: typewriter, thock, bubble, glitch, rain, and musicbox and chiptune, where every letter is a fixed note so your words come out as tunes. NOTES keeps as many notes as you like, each saved as you type, titled by its first line, with a list beside the page. CODE takes JavaScript, which runs inside the window, or Python and Bash, which run in your terminal, and runs whatever you wrote with Ctrl+Enter: broken code doesn't run and says so, valid code runs full-screen. You can start from a blank page (Enter indents after a brace, a closing brace steps back out) or type one of 32 programs over its faint ghost — from easy to extreme: a rainbow snake, a spinning cube, boids, metaballs, a mandelbrot dive, the spinning ASCII donut, doom fire — and press Ctrl+F to finish it and go straight to changing it. Programs can be played: while one runs, the keyboard and the mouse belong to it (keys, mouse, onKey, onClick), and only Esc or the stop button bring you back — so there are games too: snake, pong, flappy dot, a swarm that chases your mouse, and a paint program. Long lines scroll the page sideways instead of running off the edge. LYRICS takes whatever is playing in Spotify, YouTube or mpv, or a file from your Music folder, finds its lyrics and makes them the ghost. In follow flow the song plays through the line you're on and waits at the start of the next one until you get there. Finishing a line and typing on drops you onto the next one. SPEED is a typing test. Pick 15, 30, 60 or 120 seconds, infinite, or a custom time, and random words, sentences, hard text or your own page. Your speed is live at the bottom the whole time, with accuracy, the clock and a sparkline. A timed run ends on a results card that keeps your best for each combination. In LYRICS and SPEED every key fills the next letter's slot and the line never moves: a wrong letter stays red where it is and you carry on, extra letters are refused, and a space mid-word skips to the next word. In CODE, mistakes are counted word by word, so one slip costs one word. Every setting is in the F6 panel: it applies live while you type, the wheel scrolls it, Delete resets one setting, and a row at the bottom resets them all.
